WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.639
Welcome to this explainer on character
encoding.

2
00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:07.179
Okay, so in our first session, we
absolutely nailed number bases and binary

3
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.900
arithmetic. You already know exactly how
computers crunch numbers using just ones

4
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.440
and zeros. But today, we're going to build
on that knowledge and figure out exactly

5
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.359
how computers manage to store, sort, and
understand the actual text you type.

6
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.879
How do we get from a letter on a screen to
a binary number in a processor?

7
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.480
Let's find out. Here is the roadmap for
today's revision.

8
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:35.399
Translating text, the ASCII standard,
calculating codes, the Unicode solution,

9
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.320
ASCII versus Unicode, and finally, sorting
data.

10
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.159
But before we really dive in, just look
down at your keyboard for a second.

11
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.220
How on earth does the physical tap of a
plastic key actually transform into those

12
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.799
electrical ones and zeros we tackled in
lesson one?

13
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.159
There's got to be some kind of middle
step, right?

14
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.359
A translator? Section one, translating
text and the core problem we face.

15
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.940
Well, since computers literally only
understand binary, we need a bulletproof,

16
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:08.340
universally agreed upon system to convert
our letters, punctuation, and symbols into

17
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.720
numbers. And this is exactly what we call
a character set.

18
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.199
You definitely want to memorize this
definition for your exam.

19
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.079
It's the agreed list that maps each
character to its unique binary code.

20
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.639
It's kind of like a secret decoder ring.
Every single letter you type gets assigned

21
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.399
a unique number, which is then stored as
its binary equivalent.

22
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.019
Without this shared set, a file you make
on your laptop would just look like

23
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.360
absolute gibberish if you emailed it to a
friend.

24
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.120
Moving on to section two, the ASCII
standard, our 7-bit pioneer.

25
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.279
So early computer scientists tackled this
exact translation problem with something

26
00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:44.519
called the American Standard Code for
Information Interchange, or as we all call

27
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.639
it, ASCII. It gave early computers a
common language, and by using exactly 7

28
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:56.280
bits per character, it gave them exactly
128 unique codes, ranging from 0 to 127.

29
00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.039
Why 128? Well, 2 to the power of 7 is 128.

30
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.159
These 128 characters cover the basics.

31
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.239
Uppercase and lowercase alphabet, digits 0
through 9, basic punctuation, and a few

32
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.040
control things like hitting the enter key
for a new line.

33
00:02:11.919 --> 00:02:14.840
Okay, listen up, because this is
absolutely crucial.

34
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.400
You literally must memorize these three
magic starting numbers for your exam.

35
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.519
Uppercase A is 65, lowercase A is 97, and
the digit 0 is 48.

36
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.659
Why is this such a big deal? Because the
whole genius behind ASCII is that the

37
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.719
codes are grouped and perfectly
sequential.

38
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.759
If A is 65, it just naturally follows that
B is 66, C is 67, and so on.

39
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.040
If you know one code in a group, you can
figure out the entire rest of the set.

40
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:48.199
Section 3, calculating codes, or as I like
to call it, exam detective work.

41
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.920
Because these codes are perfectly
sequential, AQA examiners love, and I mean

42
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.679
love, asking you to calculate character
codes.

43
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.800
It's easy points once you know the trick.
Let's say your exam asks for the ASCII

44
00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.720
code for an uppercase G. Step one,
identify the starting code.

45
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.639
We know A is 65, right? Step two,
calculate the offset.

46
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.839
A is the first letter, G is the seventh.
That means G is exactly six letters away

47
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.520
from A. Step three, just add that offset
to the starting code.

48
00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.080
65 plus 6 equals 71. Boom, G is 71.

49
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.800
And you know, it works backwards too. If
an exam says a character has the code 100,

50
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.199
just remember lowercase a is 97. 100 minus
97 is 3.

51
00:03:30.639 --> 00:03:33.840
Count three letters forward from a, b, c,
d.

52
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.680
The character is lowercase d. Now this
brings us to a notorious exam trap.

53
00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.139
I see this all the time. The text
character of a number is absolutely not

54
00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.280
the same as the mathematical value of that
number.

55
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.000
Remember earlier, the text character for
the digit 0 starts at ASCII code 40.

56
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.300
So counting up seven spots means the text
character seven actually has an ASCII code

57
00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.760
of 55. It is completely 100% different
from the mathematical integer seven that

58
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.439
you'd use in, say, an addition problem.
All right, section four, the Unicode

59
00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.080
solution, our modern hero. So while ASCII
was awesome for early English-speaking

60
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.460
scientists, it had a massive flaw.
Absolutely zero space for accented

61
00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.339
letters, Chinese characters, Arabic
script, or, you know, the emojis you use

62
00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:22.239
every single day. ASCII just couldn't
handle it.

63
00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:27.740
Enter Unicode. Unicode was developed using
more bits per character to solve exactly

64
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.519
this problem. By doing that, Unicode
completely blew past that tiny 128

65
00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.800
character limit, giving us a massive,
mind-blowing capacity of over 1 million

66
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.519
distinct characters. That's literally
enough for every single writing system

67
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.199
currently in use on planet Earth, plus
dead historical scripts, obscure

68
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.880
mathematical symbols, and yes, literally
every emoji you can think of.

69
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.799
Okay, this next concept is a massive
keyword for AQA, so remember this:

70
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.320
backwards compatible. When they designed
Unicode, they made a genius decision.

71
00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.639
Unicode uses exactly the same codes as
ASCII for the first 128 characters.

72
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.119
Uppercase A is still 65 in Unicode,
exactly like it was in ASCII.

73
00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.480
This backwards compatibility means an old
ASCII text file from, say, the 1980s can

74
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.899
be read seamlessly by a modern Unicode
system today, without any translation or

75
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.579
conversion needed whatsoever. Moving to
section 5, ASCII versus Unicode and the

76
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:26.839
inevitable trade-off. Let's distill all
this down so you can easily weigh the pros

77
00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.199
and cons in your exam. It's basically a
trade-off.

78
00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.279
ASCII uses just 7 bits per character,
which means it has very low storage

79
00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.339
requirements. It's incredibly efficient if
you literally only need basic English

80
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.600
text. Unicode, on the other hand, uses
more bits per character to pull off its 1

81
00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.519
million plus capacity. So the trade-off
here is storage space.

82
00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.539
A Unicode text file is always going to
require more storage space per character

83
00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.199
than an ASCII file. But honestly, on
modern hardware where we have gigabytes

84
00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.399
and terabytes of memory, this trade-off is
absolutely worth it to enable true global

85
00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.680
communication. And that's exactly why
virtually every modern browser and

86
00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:02.079
operating system just defaults to Unicode
now.

87
00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:08.399
Finally, section 6, sorting data,
specifically using lexicographic order.

88
00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.119
So think about when you click sort
alphabetically in a spreadsheet.

89
00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.679
What is the computer actually doing behind
the scenes?

90
00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.579
Well, computers don't inherently
understand the alphabet.

91
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.820
They just understand numbers, right? So
they sort text by comparing the character

92
00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.800
codes numerically. We call this
lexicographic order.

93
00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.439
A comes before B for the simple fact that
65 is less than 66.

94
00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.279
But here's the crazy part. Because all
uppercase letters are grouped from 65 to

95
00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.920
90, and lowercase letters don't even start
until 97, a simple computer sort will

96
00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:42.040
shockingly put a capital Z before a
lowercase A.

97
00:06:42.279 --> 00:06:45.639
Why? Because 90 is less than 97. It's as
simple as that.

98
00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:49.620
And actually, this brilliantly explains
that classic programming glitch you've

99
00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.720
probably seen, where a list weirdly sorts
to number 10 before the number 9.

100
00:06:54.279 --> 00:06:57.779
If the computer thinks it's sorting text
instead of math, it just looks at the very

101
00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:02.319
first character of the string. The text
character 1 has an ASCII code of 49.

102
00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.560
The text character 9 has an ASCII code of
57.

103
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.139
Because 49 is less than 57, the computer
confidently just slaps 10 right at the top

104
00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:13.899
of the list. Understanding these character
sets explains exactly why these weird

105
00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.839
little bugs happen. You know, you have now
totally mastered how we translate simple

106
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.040
text into binary ones and zeros using
character sets.

107
00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:26.819
But as we look ahead to our next explainer
in this data representation unit, I really

108
00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.920
want you to start wondering about
something big.

109
00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.100
If it takes this much thought and
processing just to turn the letter A into

110
00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:37.320
a number, what kind of mathematical magic
do we need to turn a vibrant million-color

111
00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:41.579
photograph into pure binary? Keep that
rattling around in your head, and I'll see

112
00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.579
you in the next lesson where we conquer
representing images and sound.

113
00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:45.399
See you then.
